ch=0A=0AWe have talked <https://bloom.bg/4i4QPfY> a few <https://bloom.bg/4=
hKzYxF> =0Atimes <https://bloom.bg/4AQQEw1> about <https://bloom.bg/4zDEQw3=
> the basis =0Atrade <https://bloom.bg/4yc1MRL>. What the basis trade is, e=
conomically, is =0Athat big asset managers want to own interest-rate durati=
on through Treasury =0Afutures. A pension fund or endowment might have to m=
ake payments over the next =0A30 years, so it might want to own assets that=
 also pay out over 30 years. It =0Acould buy a portfolio of 30-year US Trea=
sury bonds to perfectlymatch its assets =0Aand liabilities <https://bloom.b=
g/4y1j6sv>, but that would be expensive: =0ATreasury bonds are safe assets,=
 so they don=E2=80=99t yield very much. Instead, it will =0Aprobably buy a =
portfolio of other stuff =E2=80=94 corporate bonds, private credit, etc. =
=0A=E2=80=94 with more credit risk and higher yields. But that stuff tends =
to have shorter =0Amaturities than long-term Treasury bonds, and would leav=
e the manager exposed =0Ato a lot of risk if interest rates go down. [1]  <=
>=0A=0ATo get the right level of interest-rate risk, the asset manager migh=
t then buy =0Asome Treasury futures contracts, derivatives contracts tied t=
o the prices of =0ATreasury bonds. The futures contracts allow the asset ma=
nager to effectively =0Aown long-term Treasuries, without paying for them y=
et. And then the basis trade =0Ais that hedge funds will buy the underlying=
 Treasury bonds, sell the Treasury =0Afutures to asset managers, and collec=
t a spread for their trouble.=0A=0ALet=E2=80=99s say that the asset manager=
 buys the corporate bonds she wants, and then =0Asolves for how much Treasu=
ry futures she needs to get the duration she wants. =0AShe might do the mat=
h and decide she wants to own, say, $100 million of 20-year =0ATreasury fut=
ures. Or maybe she wants a bit less duration, so she buys 15-year =0ATreasu=
ry futures. Or a bit more, so she buys 25-year Treasury futures. Fine, =0Ay=
es, good.=0A=0AThe problem is that =E2=80=9C20-year Treasury futures,=E2=80=
=9D or =E2=80=9C15-year Treasury futures=E2=80=9D =0Aor =E2=80=9C25-year Tr=
easury futures,=E2=80=9D are not quite things. Instead there is a thing =0A=
called the =E2=80=9CTreasury bond futures contract,=E2=80=9D which has the =
following odd =0Aproperty:=0A=0A * When long-term Treasury interest rates a=
re lower than 6%, it is a 15-year =0ATreasury futures contract. =0A * When =
long-term Treasury interest rates are higher than 6%, it is a 25-year =0ATr=
easury futures contract. =0AThis makes sense in its context. The context is=
 that the Treasury bond future =0Ais, approximately, a futures contract on =
an imaginary object that is a 20-year =0ATreasury bond with a 6% yield. Fro=
m Frank Fabozzi=E2=80=99s Handbook of of Fixed Income =0ASecurities <https:=
//bloom.bg/4rMW9I4> [2]  <>: =0A=0AThe Treasury bond (T-bond) futures contr=
act is the most successful =0Ainterest-rate (or commodity) futures contract=
. Prices and yields on the T-bond =0Afutures contract are quoted in terms =
of a (fictitious) 20-year 6% Treasury =0Abond, but the exchange where the c=
ontract is traded, the Chicago Mercantile =0AExchange (CME), allows many di=
fferent bonds to be delivered in satisfaction of =0Aa short position in the=
 contract. Specifically, any Treasury bond with a =0Aremaining maturity of =
at least 15 years, but less than 25 years, from the first =0Aday of the del=
ivery month is acceptable for delivery. There is an Ultra Long =0ATreasury =
futures contract that differs only in that it is based on Treasury =0Abonds=
 with at least 25 years to maturity.=0A=0AThe T-bond futures contract calls=
 for the short (i.e., the seller) to deliver =0A$100,000 face value of any =
one of the qualifying Treasury bonds. However, =0Abecause the coupons and m=
aturities vary widely, the practice is that the buyer =0Apays the seller de=
pends on which bond the seller chooses to deliver. The rule =0Aused by the =
CME is one that adjusts the futures price by a conversion factor =0Athat re=
flects the price the bond would sell for at the beginning of the =0Adeliver=
y month if it were yielding 6%.=0A=0ASo if you buy the Treasury futures con=
tract, you are agreeing to take delivery =0Aof a bond of the seller=E2=80=
=99s choosing, and to pay the seller an amount of cash =0Athat depends on t=
he bond it chooses. You can get a list of the eligible bonds, =0Aand their =
conversion factors, at Bloomberg page USA Comdty DLV. [3]  <> There =0Aare =
59 bonds on the list. The first bond on the list matures in February 2045 =
=0A(about 18 years from the futures expiry in December) and has a coupon of=
 2.5%. =0AYou can do some simple bond math to figure out the price that you=
=E2=80=99d have to pay =0Afor an 18-year bond with a 2.5% coupon to make it=
 yield 6%, and the answer is =0A61.79 cents on the dollar, [4]  <> which is=
 in fact the conversion factor =0A(0.6179) that Bloomberg shows. The last b=
ond on the list matures in May 2050 =0A(about 23 years) and has a coupon of=
 1.25%; its conversion factor is 0.4086.=0A=0AThe December Treasury bond fu=
tures contract <https://bloom.bg/3WbEbmL> closed =0Ayesterday at about $102=
,188. [5]  <> So you can enter into a contract now in =0Awhich:=0A=0A * In =
December, a hedge fund will deliver you $100,000 face amount of the =0ATrea=
sury bond of its choosing (with between 15 and 25 years of remaining =0Amat=
urity), and =0A * In December, you will pay that hedge fund $102,188 times =
the relevant =0Aconversion factor for the bond it delivers. [6]  <> =0ASo t=
he hedge fund could deliver the 2.5% bond of 2045 and get back $63,141 =0A(=
0.6179 times $102,188), or it could deliver the 1.25% of 2050 and get back =
=0A$41,754 (0.4086 times $102,188). Or any of the other 57 eligible bonds, =
at =0Atheir particular conversion factors.=0A=0AWhich will it deliver=3F We=
ll, it depends on the trading prices of the bonds. If =0Ait can buy a bond =
for $41,500 and deliver it for $41,754, that=E2=80=99s better than =0Abuyin=
g a different bond for $63,200 and delivering it for $63,141. This is a =0A=
liquid and efficient market, and the hedge fund will deliver the =0A=E2=80=
=9Ccheapest-to-deliver=E2=80=9D bond, that is, the one trading at the lowes=
t price =0Arelative to its futures payoff.=0A=0AAnd so, locally, the Treasu=
ry bond futures contract is a contract on the =0Acheapest-to-deliver bond. =
If the cheapest-to-deliver bond is the 2.5% bond of =0A2045, then buying Tr=
easury bond futures gives you exposure to that bond: The =0Aprice of the fu=
tures contract will move up and down with the price of that =0Aparticular b=
ond.=0A=0AWhy would one bond be cheaper to deliver than another=3F The roug=
h intuition is =0Athat the trading prices of Treasury bonds reflect the pre=
sent value of their =0Acash flows discounted at the current long-term Treas=
ury rate. [7]  <> (Right =0Anow an actual 20-year Treasury bond yields abou=
t 5.70%.) But the futures =0Aconversion factor for each bond reflects the p=
resent value of its cash flows =0Adiscounted at 6% [8]  <>; it turns every =
Treasury bond into a synthetic bond =0Ayielding 6%.=0A=0AIf the market inte=
rest rate is significantly below 6%, then shorter-dated =0Abonds will be ch=
eaper to deliver. [9]  <> Intuitively, if long-term interest =0Arates are 5=
%, then a 6% interest rate is above-market, and you=E2=80=99d like to get =
=0Apaid that rate as long as possible. So a synthetic 6% bond is more valua=
ble as =0Aits duration is longer, and the shortest-duration synthetic 6% bo=
nd is the =0Aleast valuable and so the cheapest to deliver.=0A=0AIf the mar=
ket interest rate is significantly above 6%, then longer-dated bonds =0Awil=
l be cheaper to deliver. Intuitively, if long-term interest rates are 7%, =
=0Athen a 6% interest rate is below-market, and you=E2=80=99d rather get pa=
id that rate =0Afor the shortest possible time. So a synthetic 6% bond is m=
ore valuable as its =0Aduration is shorter, and the longest-duration synthe=
tic 6% bond is the least =0Avaluable and so the cheapest to deliver. =E2=80=
=9CWhen yields are below 6%, the =0Acheapest-to-deliver bond is typically o=
ne of the lower duration eligible bonds, =0Aand when yields are higher than=
 6%, the cheapest-to-deliver would typically be =0Aa longer-duration bond,=
=E2=80=9D says Fabozzi. [10]  <>=0A=0AAnd if market interest rates have bee=
n significantly below 6% for decades, but =0Aare now approaching 6% and mig=
ht cross over, you get some weird stuff. =0ABloomberg=E2=80=99sEdward Bolin=
gbroke reports <https://bloom.bg/4rMHaOg>:=0A=0AAs US 30-year securities he=
ad ever closer toward 6%, the move threatens a =0Ashakeup in futures, which=
 are widely used by investors to hedge government bond =0Apositions, and by=
 leveraged funds in strategies such as the popular =E2=80=9Cbasis =0Atrade.=
=E2=80=9D=0A=0AIt has to do with the mechanics. US bond futures are exchang=
e-traded =0Aagreements to buy or sell Treasuries at a set price and date. T=
hey=E2=80=99re governed =0Aby contract terms that dictate what type of unde=
rlying securities are allowed =0Ato be delivered by traders with short posi=
tions to those with long positions, =0Aand several typically qualify. Trade=
rs then identify the one that=E2=80=99s cheapest to =0Adeliver, or =E2=80=
=9CCTD,=E2=80=9D and the price of the futures contract tracks it.=0A=0AWhen=
 yields rise rapidly, as is happening now, the pricing dynamics of the =0Ad=
eliverable basket of securities are altered, and the CTD starts to migrate =
=0Atoward a longer-maturity bond. This forces asset managers to adjust to t=
his =0Aso-called duration shift, or =E2=80=9CCTD switch,=E2=80=9D by sellin=
g Treasury futures, which =0Ain turn =E2=80=9Ccould exacerbate the rise in =
long-end yields=E2=80=9D in the cash market, =0Aaccording to a recent note =
by strategists at BNP, including Guneet Dhingra, =0ASebastian Mauleon and V=
incent Zhou.=0A=0ATo maintain a stable duration target in a portfolio as th=
e CTD shifts toward =0Alonger-duration bonds, long futures positions would =
need to be sold while short =0Apositions would need to be bought back. Depe=
nding on how these dynamics =0Ainteract and at what times, this can cause s=
pillover effects in the underlying =0Abonds.=0A=0AFocus is on the longer ma=
turities, notably the long-bond contract where the =0Acurrent CTD security =
is the 2.5% February 2045 bond. A Bloomberg scenario =0Aanalysis shows that=
 a 30-year yield rise to near 6% could result in a CTD shift =0Ato a 2050 m=
aturity. =E2=80=A6=0A=0AIn a note on Monday, Barclays PLC strategists Andre=
s Mok and Amrut Nashikka =0Ahighlighted the duration implications around CT=
D extensions for asset managers, =0Anoting that =E2=80=9Cinvestors may need=
 to reduce futures exposure or rebalance hedge =0Aratios to bring portfolio=
 duration back in line with their targets.=E2=80=9D They added =0Athat =E2=
=80=9Chigher rates and volatility have increased uncertainty around CTD =0A=
outcomes.=E2=80=9D They estimate asset managers=E2=80=99 gross rebalancing =
needs at about $25 =0Amillion per basis point in the long-bond contract, an=
d $3 million per basis =0Apoint in the ultra-long bond contract.=0A=0A=E2=
=80=9CThe negative convexity profile inherent in long-end futures contracts=
 can =0Ainduce further duration selling during selloffs,=E2=80=9D say those=
 BNP strategists. =0ABasically, right now, a lot of asset managers own a lo=
t of 18-year Treasury =0Afutures. If rates get above 6%, they will suddenly=
 own a lot of 23-year =0ATreasury futures. [11]  <> This might give them mo=
re duration than they wanted. =0ASo they might unwind some futures trades (=
by selling the futures), which might =0Acause hedge funds to unwind their b=
asis trades (by selling the bonds), which =0Amight cause rates to go higher=
.=0A=0A =0A<https://sli.bloomberg.com/click=3Fs=3D868432&stpe=3Ddefault&li=
