=0ADefeasance=0A=0AIn February 2021, Electronic Arts Inc. issued $750 milli=
on of 30-year bonds =0A<https://bloom.bg/4r5nYdw> (due in 2051) that paid i=
nterest of 2.95%. (It also =0Aissued some 10-year bonds, due in 2031.) Elec=
tronic Arts could not issue =0A30-year bonds that pay 2.95% today, for at l=
east two reasons:.=0A=0A * Interest rates went up. When EA issued that bond=
, a 30-year US Treasury bond=0A <https://bloom.bg/3TCpgBn> paid about 1.95%=
 interest, so EA was paying about =0A100 basis points over Treasuries. Toda=
y, a 30-year Treasury bond yields about =0A5.64%; at the same spread, EA wo=
uld have to pay about 6.64%. =0A * EA=E2=80=99s credit got worse. When EA i=
ssued that bond, it got investment-grade =0Acredit ratings of BBB+ from Sta=
ndard & Poor=E2=80=99s and Baa1 from Moody=E2=80=99s. But this =0Ayear, EA =
went private in a $55 billion leveraged buyout thatclosed in August =0A<htt=
ps://bloom.bg/4rS9Mpu>. Leveraged buyouts ordinarily increase the leverage =
=0Aof a company and, thus, make its debt riskier. =E2=80=9CMoody=E2=80=99s =
withdrew its rating on =0Athe bonds,=E2=80=9Dreports the Wall Street Journa=
l <https://bloom.bg/4jGXBtw>, =E2=80=9Cand S&P =0Aindicated it would rate t=
hem high-yield, reflecting the additional debt on EA=E2=80=99s =0Abalance s=
heet following its buyout.=E2=80=9D =0AThe first effect is much, much more =
economically important than the second. In =0Around numbers, if EA=E2=80=99=
s credit had stayed the same, its 30-year bond would have =0Alost almost ha=
lf its value anyway; it would be trading at about 56 cents on the =0Adollar=
, just because of how much interest rates went up. For reasons we=E2=80=99l=
l get =0Ato, the bonds never actually traded below about 74 cents on the do=
llar. =0ABasically all the value that EA bondholders lost =E2=80=94 actuall=
y more than all of it =0A=E2=80=94 came from interest-rate moves, not credi=
t moves.=0A=0AThe credit moves are arguably more legally important, though.=
 Typically, when =0Aa company gets acquired in an LBO, that is bad for its =
bondholders, so the =0Abondholders usually have protections. The classic pr=
otection is a =0Achange-of-control put at 101: If the company has a change =
of control (like an =0ALBO) that causes it to lose its investment-grade rat=
ings, the bondholders can =0Ademand that the company buy their bonds back a=
t 101 cents on the dollar. EA=E2=80=99s =0Abonds have that provision. [12] =
 <>=0A=0AAnd so the LBO should have been a huge win for bondholders. If EA =
had just =0Abopped along being a BBB+ public company, its 30-year bonds wou=
ld have been =0Aworth about 56 cents on the dollar; the bondholders would h=
ave lost a ton of =0Amoney on interest-rate moves. But because it got acqui=
red and downgraded, the =0Abondholders can demand 101 cents on the dollar r=
ight now, an 80% improvement in =0Atheir situation.=0A=0AExcept! In theory,=
 the point of the change-of-control put is to protect =0Abondholders fromcr=
edit risk (the LBO made the credit worse), not interest-rate =0Arisk. And t=
here is, in bond lore, another, weirder way to protect bondholders =0Afrom =
credit risk. It is called =E2=80=9Cdefeasance.=E2=80=9D Idescribed it this =
February =0A<https://bloom.bg/4hvnjQl>:=0A=0ATo defease a bond, a company d=
eposits all of the future interest and principal =0Apayments on the bond wi=
th the bond=E2=80=99s trustee, and then it washes its hands of =0Athe bond.=
 Roughly speaking, the bond is no longer the company=E2=80=99s problem: The=
 =0Atrustee has all the money it needs to make all the payments, so the com=
pany is =0Aoff the hook. All [the bond=E2=80=99s] covenants =E2=80=94 about=
 not borrowing more money or =0Adoing mergers =E2=80=94 no longer apply, be=
cause the bondholders no longer need to =0Aworry about the company=E2=80=99=
s credit, because the money is already there.=0A=0AThere is one important s=
implification in the previous paragraph: I said that =0Athe =E2=80=9Ccompan=
y deposits all of the future interest and principal payments=E2=80=9D with =
=0Athe trustee, but it normally doesn=E2=80=99t do that in cash. It normall=
y does it in US =0ATreasury securities.=0A=0AEA=E2=80=99s bonds do have a d=
efeasance provision. [13]  <> Here, in round numbers, =0AElectronic Arts co=
uld deposit Treasuries worth about 65 cents on the dollar =0Awith its trust=
ee, [14]  <> and those Treasuries would produce enough cash flows =0Ato pay=
 all of the interest and principal on its 2051 bonds. And that would =0Atot=
ally solve the bondholders=E2=80=99 credit worries: Despite EA=E2=80=99s ne=
w owners, =0Aincreased leverage and lower credit ratings, bondholders can b=
e pretty =0Aconfident that they=E2=80=99ll get paid back, because the trust=
ee has risk-free =0ATreasury bonds sufficient to make all of their payments=
.=0A=0AEA would obviously rather defease these bonds, at a cost of 65 cent=
s on the =0Adollar, than pay them back at 101 cents on the dollar. The bond=
holders would =0Aobviously rather get 101 cents on the dollar. TheWall Stre=
et Journal reports =0A<https://bloom.bg/4jGXBtw>:=0A=0AElectronic Arts bond=
holders alleged a $1.4 billion debt default, escalating a =0Adispute over w=
hether the videogame maker must pay them off at a premium after =0Agoing pr=
ivate in the largest leveraged buyout of all time.=0A=0AAn investor group n=
otified the company of the alleged default following the =0Acompletion of E=
A=E2=80=99s take-private by a consortium including Saudi Arabia=E2=80=99s P=
ublic =0AInvestment Fund, private-equity firm Silver Lake and Jared Kushner=
=E2=80=99s Affinity =0APartners. Bondholders asserted they must be repaid a=
t 101 cents on the dollar, =0Aa common redemption premium after a corporate=
 borrower undergoes a change in =0Acontrol, according to a bondholder commu=
nication sent last week and reviewed by =0AWSJ Pro.=0A=0AEA didn=E2=80=99t =
pay the change-in-control premium after its buyout closed in August, =0Ains=
tead attempting to defease the bonds by setting aside U.S. Treasurys to =0A=
cover future interest and principal payments. =E2=80=A6=0A=0ABondholders re=
volted, signing a cooperation agreement pledging to act in =0Aunison and re=
taining law firm Akin Gump Strauss Hauer & Feld and financial =0Aadviser Ho=
ulihan Lokey.=0A=0ABasically the question here is which provision of the bo=
nds controls. The =0Abondholders think that, if there=E2=80=99s a change of=
 control and a downgrade, EA has =0Ato buy back the bonds at 101; it doesn=
=E2=80=99t have the option to defease them. EA =0Athinks that, if it does t=
he defeasance, then the change of control put is moot. =0A[15]  <>=0A=0AWho=
=E2=80=99s right=3F As a matter of what the bond contract says, I=E2=80=99m=
 not sure. But what=0Ashouldit say=3F On the one hand, the point of the cha=
nge-of-control put really is =0Ato protect the bondholders from thecredit r=
isk of an LBO, and arguably if EA =0Acan solve that credit risk then the bo=
ndholders shouldn=E2=80=99t complain.=0A=0AOn the other hand, the point of =
the change-of-control put arguably is, a =0Alittle bit, to deliver windfall=
s to bondholders on changes of control: =0AInvestors are more likely to buy=
 30-year bonds of video game companies in =0Asuper-low-interest-rate enviro=
nments if they think =E2=80=9Cyou know, we have a lot of =0Ainterest-rate r=
isk here, but 30 years is a long time, and there=E2=80=99s a decent =0Achan=
ce that the company will be acquired and have to pay back the bonds early =
=0Ain a way that hands us a big windfall.=E2=80=9D The change-of-control pu=
t might have =0Abeen an important part of what bondholders were buying, and=
 it might not be =0Afair for them not to get it.=0A=0A=0AEmerging asset cla=
sses=0A=0AI feel so dumb and annoying writing this, but all of it is obviou=
sly true:=0A=0A * Betting on sports is not an investment <https://bloom.bg/=
3UaXIDi>. Not in =0Asome subjective sense =E2=80=94 =E2=80=9Cyou=E2=80=99ll=
 probably lose,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cit=E2=80=99s too risky,=E2=80=9D etc. =E2=
=80=94 but in =0Athe sense that it is zero-sum. If you invest in stocks or =
bonds, money will =0Aflow to companies which will use it for productive pur=
poses and create economic =0Agrowth, in which you will share. If you bet on=
 sports, either you will win =0Amoney from someone else or someone else wil=
l win money from you. There is no =0Aactual investment involved, and the pi=
e does not grow. The aggregate expected =0Areturn from sports betting has t=
o be zero, whereas the aggregate expected =0Areturn to stock-market investi=
ng is positive. =0A * Betting on sports is, however, a great business for b=
ookmakers: The =0Aaggregatereturn from sports betting is zero, but in pract=
ice that means that =0Auninformed retail sports bettors reliably lose money=
 to professional =0Abookmakers. To the extent that sports betting is legal =
and popular, the people =0Awho provide the sports bets do well. =0A * Since=
 2024, a lot of people in and around the financial industry have =0Abecome =
very interested in pushing the notion that actually betting on sportsis =0A=
an investment. The US Commodity Futures Trading Commission has pushed toexp=
and =0Asports betting <https://bloom.bg/4hvngnD> nationwide and do it on re=
gulated =0Afinancial markets, Kalshi and Polymarket have become big sports-=
betting =0Abusinesses using the language of financial markets, there=E2=80=
=99s a (proposed)sports =0Agambling exchange-traded fund <https://bloom.bg/=
4i6XRAM>, and Robinhood, the =0Abig brokerage that helps a lot of people sa=
ve for retirement, has called sports =0Agambling =E2=80=9Can emerging asset=
 class <https://bloom.bg/4zcVHVZ>.=E2=80=9D =0A * Points 2 and 3 are connec=
ted in ways that are so obvious I won=E2=80=99t bother =0Adiscussing them. =
=0A * This push has worked, and now lots of people believe that betting on =
sports =0Ais an investment, and that they should allocate a portion of thei=
r retirement =0Asavings to sports betting. =0A * This is obviously bad! =0A=
 * Come on! =0ACNBC reports <https://bloom.bg/4i3YuLs>:=0A=0AA survey of re=
tail investors released in August by Betterment, an investment =0Aadvisory =
platform, found that 66% of Gen Z investors participate in sports =0Abettin=
g. The Bank of America Institute found in a September report that Gen Z =0A=
made up almost 50% of all online betting activity in July, during the heigh=
t of =0Athe 2026 FIFA World Cup, outnumbering millennials for the first tim=
e. =E2=80=A6=0A=0AThe Bank of America Institute survey found that Gen Z was=
 twice as likely to =0Asee sports betting as a type of investment, versus 2=
0% of respondents overall. =0AFor prediction markets alone, respondents ove=
rall saw them as a form of =0Ainvesting, but those numbers were again highe=
r for Gen Z.=0A=0AIn Betterment=E2=80=99s retail investor survey, 52% of Ge=
n Z respondents said they =0Amoved money originally meant for investment to=
 sports betting, while another =0A26% saw wagering as a part of their long-=
term financial strategy. =E2=80=A6=0A=0ADan Egan, director of behavioral fi=
nance and investing at Betterment, said =0Asports betting increasingly appe=
ars alongside traditional investments on the =0Asame app or device, helping=
 drive the association.=0A=0AThe conflation is concerning because of the hi=
ghly active behavior required to =0Amanage wagers on sports, unlike a long-=
term investment, Egan said.=0A=0A=E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s not an asset that gr=
ows with the economy, that kind of gets better as =0Atime goes on, that has=
 a positive expected return, and that you can kind of sit =0Aback and not h=
ave to do anything with,=E2=80=9D he said. =E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s the exact =
opposite.=E2=80=9D=0A=0AI feel like, five years ago, nobody would have foun=
d it necessary to spell out =0Athat sports gambling is the exact opposite o=
f investing. Of course sports =0Agambling is the opposite of investing! Eve=
ryone has always known that! But now =0Athey don=E2=80=99t.=0A=0A=0AExisten=
tial risk=0A=0AApparently the prospectus for the upcoming initial public of=
fering of =0AAnthropic PBC contains a list of risk factors including =E2=80=
