=9Cbasis trade.=E2=80=9D</p> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">It has to do with=
 the mechanics. US bond futures are exchange-traded agreements to buy or se=
ll Treasuries at a set price and date. They=E2=80=99re governed by contract=
 terms that dictate what type of underlying securities are allowed to be de=
livered by traders with short positions to those with long positions, and s=
everal typically qualify. Traders then identify the one that=E2=80=99s chea=
pest to deliver, or =E2=80=9CCTD,=E2=80=9D and the price of the futures con=
tract tracks it.</p> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">When yields rise rapidly,=
 as is happening now, the pricing dynamics of the deliverable basket of sec=
urities are altered, and the CTD starts to migrate toward a longer-maturity=
 bond. This forces asset managers to adjust to this so-called duration shif=
t, or =E2=80=9CCTD switch,=E2=80=9D by selling Treasury futures, which in t=
urn =E2=80=9Ccould exacerbate the rise in long-end yields=E2=80=9D in the c=
ash market, according to a recent note by strategists at BNP, including Gun=
eet Dhingra, Sebastian Mauleon and Vincent Zhou.</p> <p style=3D"margin: 16=
px 0;">To maintain a stable duration target in a portfolio as the CTD shift=
s toward longer-duration bonds, long futures positions would need to be sol=
d while short positions would need to be bought back. Depending on how thes=
e dynamics interact and at what times, this can cause spillover effects in =
the underlying bonds.</p> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">Focus is on the long=
