y is the 2.5% February 2045 bond. A Bloomberg scenario analysis shows that =
a 30-year yield rise to near 6% could result in a CTD shift to a 2050 matur=
ity. =E2=80=A6</p> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">In a note on Monday, Barcla=
ys PLC strategists Andres Mok and Amrut Nashikka highlighted the duration i=
mplications around CTD extensions for asset managers, noting that =E2=80=9C=
investors may need to reduce futures exposure or rebalance hedge ratios to =
bring portfolio duration back in line with their targets.=E2=80=9D They add=
ed that =E2=80=9Chigher rates and volatility have increased uncertainty aro=
und CTD outcomes.=E2=80=9D They estimate asset managers=E2=80=99 gross reba=
lancing needs at about $25 million per basis point in the long-bond contrac=
t, and $3 million per basis point in the ultra-long bond contract.</p> </bl=
ockquote> <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CThe negative convexity prof=
ile inherent in long-end futures contracts can induce further duration sell=
ing during selloffs,=E2=80=9D say those BNP strategists. Basically, right n=
